stem_md:
Let  $a$  and  $b$  be positive integers such that
(i) both  $a$  and  $b$  have at least two digits;
(ii)  $a + b$  is divisible by  $10$ ;
(iii)  $a$  can be changed into  $b$  by changing its last digit.
Prove that the hundreds digit of the product  $ab$  is even.

solution_md:
Official solution from the booklet. **Solution 1.** The only solutions are  $f(x)=x$  for all  $x\neq 0$  and  $f(0)\le 0$  is arbitrary, when  $c=3$ . This can be checked to work as  $f(xyz)\le xyz$ ,  $y^2f(y)=y^3$ ,  $zf(z^2)=z^3$  holds for all  $x$ ,  $y$  and  $z$  regardless of the choice of  $f(0)$ , as long as  $f(0)\le 0$ . The verification then follows from the factorization  $x^3+y^3+z^3-3xyz=\frac{1}{2}(x+y+z)\left((x-y)^2+(y-z)^2+(z-x)^2\right)$ . We now show that these are the only solutions.**Part I:** First we will prove that if such a function  $f$  exists, then  $c=3$  must hold. 

*Claim 1:*  $\displaystyle\frac{f(t)}{t}\rightarrow 1$  as  $t\rightarrow +\infty$ .

*Proof:* Let  $t>0$  be any positive real number. Perform the following substitutions:  $(x,y,z)=(-t, t, 0)$  gives  $\displaystyle -t^3+t^2f(t)\ge cf(0) \Rightarrow \frac{f(t)}{t}\ge 1+\frac{cf(0)}{t^3}$  $(x,y,z)=(t, 0, -t)$  gives  $\displaystyle t^3-tf(t^2)\ge cf(0) \Rightarrow \frac{f(t^2)}{t^2}\le 1+\frac{cf(0)}{t^3}$ Since  $t^2>0$ , then   $$ 1+\frac{cf(0)}{t^6}\le \frac{f(t^2)}{t^2}\le 1+\frac{cf(0)}{t^3} $$   which proves the claim as  $t\rightarrow +\infty$ .  $\square$ *Claim 2:*  $c=3$ *Proof:* Let  $t>0$  be any positive real number. Perform the following substitutions:  $(x,y,z)=(t, t, t)$  gives  $\displaystyle t^3+t^2f(t)+tf(t^2)\ge cf(t^3) \Rightarrow 1+\frac{f(t)}{t}+\frac{f(t^2)}{t^2}\ge c\frac{f(t^3)}{t^3}$ .

Take  $t\rightarrow +\infty$ , then  $3\ge c$ .  $(x,y,z)=(t, -t, 0)$  gives  $\displaystyle t^3+t^2f(-t)\ge cf(0) \Rightarrow \frac{f(-t)}{-t}\le 1-\frac{cf(0)}{t^3}$  $(x,y,z)=(t, t, -2t)$  gives  $\displaystyle t^3+t^2f(t)-2tf(4t^2)\ge cf(-2t^3)$    $$ \Rightarrow 1+\frac{f(t)}{t}-8\left( \frac{f(4t^2)}{4t^2}\right)\ge -2c\left(\frac{f(-2t^3)}{-2t^3}\right) \ge -2c\left( 1-\frac{cf(0)}{8t^9} \right) $$   Take  $t\rightarrow +\infty$ , then  $-6\ge -2c \Rightarrow c\ge 3$ . 

Thus  $c=3$  must hold, and this completes the proof of Part I.  $\square$ **Part II:** Now we will find all functions  $f$  in the case when  $c=3$ .

Set  $x=y=z=0$  gives  $f(0)\le 0$ . Now, we start with a claim, in which we improve trivial bounds in an iterative manner.

*Claim 3:*  $f(x)\le x$  for all  $x\ge 1$ .

*Proof:* Set  $y=z=1$  we get  $x^3+2f(1)\ge 3f(x)$  for all  $x\ge -2$ . This immediately implies  $f(1)\le 1$  by setting  $x=1$  as well. In particular, this means for all  $x\ge 1$  and  $k>0$ ,  $f(1)\le 1\le x^k$ .

Now, take the sequence  $a_1=3$ ,  $a_{n+1}=\frac{2a_n+1}{3}$  for all  $n\ge 1$ . Note that  $a_n=3\cdot(\frac{2}{3})^n+1$ , which limits to  $1$ . We will prove by induction that  $f(x)\le x^{a_n}$  for all  $n\ge 1$ , then by taking limits of  $a_n$  we would prove the claim.

The base case simply follows from  $y=z=1$ , we get   $$ x^3+2f(1)\ge 3f(x) \Rightarrow f(x)\le \frac{x^3+2f(1)}{3}\le x^3 $$   for  $x\ge 1$ . Now suppose  $f(x)\le x^{a_n}$  for all  $x\ge 1$ , then plugging in  $x=y=z$  we get  $x^3+x^2f(x)+xf(x^2)\ge 3f(x^3)$  for all  $x\ge 1$ . But this implies   $$ 3f(x^3)\le x^3+x^{a_n+2}+x^{2a_n+1}\le 3x^{1+2a_n}\Rightarrow f(x^3)\le x^{3a_{n+1}} $$   for all  $x\ge 1$ . By a change of variables this means  $f(x)\le x^{a_{n+1}}$  for all  $x\ge 1$ . This proves the claim.  $\square$ *Claim 4:*  $f(x)\ge x$  for all  $x>1$ . 

*Proof:* Set  $x=-y, z=0$   we get  $y^2f(y)\ge 3f(0)+y^3$  for all  $y$ . In particular,  $f(y)\ge y+\frac{3f(0)}{y^2}$  for all non-zero reals  $y$ .

Again, take the sequence  $b_1=2$ ,  $b_{n+1}=3b_n+2$  for all  $n\ge 1$ . Note that  $b_n=3^{n}-1$ , and that  $\displaystyle \frac{3^{n}}{x^{b_n}} \rightarrow 0$  as  $n\rightarrow \infty$ , as long as  $x>1$ . We will prove by induction that   $$ f(x)\ge x+\frac{3^nf(0)}{x^{b_n}} $$   for all integers  $n\ge 1$  and reals  $x\ge 1$ . 

The base case  $n=1$  is already proved above. Suppose this is true for  $n=k$ , that is  $f(x)\ge x+\frac{3^kf(0)}{x^{b_k}}$  for all reals  $x\ge 1$ . Plugging in  $x=y=z$  we get  $x^3+x^2f(x)+xf(x^2)\ge 3f(x^3)\ge 3x^3+\frac{3^{k+1}f(0)}{x^{3b_k}}$ . But by Claim  $1$ , for all  $x>1$  we have  $x^2\ge f(x^2)$ , so   $$ 2x^3+x^2f(x)\ge x^3+x^2f(x)+xf(x^2)\ge 3x^3+\frac{3^{k+1}f(0)}{x^{3b_k}} \Rightarrow f(x)\ge x+\frac{3^{k+1}f(0)}{x^{b_{k+1}}} $$  Hence by taking  $n$  large enough,  $f(x)\ge x$  must hold for  $x>1$ . Combining with Claim  $1$  proves that  $f(x)=x$  for all  $x>1$ .  $\square$ Now we want to extend the identity domain to the rest of the non-zero reals. Till this end, the substitutions involved are standard, and invokes the identity that  $x^3+y^3+z^3=3xyz$  whenever  $x+y+z=0$ . 

*Claim 5:*  $f(x)\le x$  for all  $x>0$ .

*Proof:* Suppose  $-1\le z<0$ , take  $x<0$  small enough so that if  $y=-(x+z)$ , then  $y>1$  and  $-(x+z)xz>1$ . Then by Claim  $2$ , the inequality becomes   $$ zf(z^2)\ge 3f(xyz)-y^2f(y)-x^3=3xyz-y^3-x^3=z^3 $$   Thus  $f(z^2)\le z^2$  because  $-1\le z<0$ . By a change of variables this implies  $f(x)\le x$  for all  $0<x\le 1$ , and thus for positive reals  $x$ .  $\square$ *Claim 6:*  $f(x)\le x$  for all  $x<0$ .

*Proof:* Let  $y>0$ ,  $z>0$  to be arbitrary, and  $x=-(y+z)$ , then using Claim  $3$  on  $f(y)$  and  $f(z^2)$  we get   $$ -3(y+z)yz=-(y+z)^3+y^3+z^3\ge -(y+z)^3+y^2f(y)+zf(z^2)\ge 3f(-(y+z)yz) $$   But  $-(y+z)yz$  runs through all negative reals as  $y, z>0$ , so  $f(x)\le x$  for all  $x<0$  as well.  $\square$ *Claim 7:*  $f(x)\ge x$  for all  $x<0$ .

*Proof:* For any  $y<0$ , take  $z<0$  small enough so that if  $x=-(y+z)>0$  then  $z^2>1$  and  $-(y+z)yz>1$ . Then by Claim  $2$ ,   $$ y^2f(y)\ge 3f(xyz)-zf(z^2)-x^3=3xyz-z^3-x^3=y^3 $$   So  $f(y)\ge y$  for all  $y<0$ .  $\square$ *Claim 8:*  $f(x)\ge x$  for all  $x>0$ .

*Proof:* For any  $y>0$ , take  $z>1$  and  $x=-(y+z)<0$ , then  $xyz<0$ , so by Claim  $2$  and  $5$    $$ y^2f(y)\ge 3f(xyz)-zf(z^2)-x^3=3xyz-z^3-x^3=y^3 $$   So  $f(y)\ge y$  for all  $y>0$ .  $\square$ We conclude that all solutions must be  $f(x)=x$  for all  $x\neq 0$ , and  $f(0)\le 0$  is arbitrary.  $\blacksquare$  **Solution 2.** (Tristan Chaang) We will focus only on the case of  $c=3$  in this solution. The case when  $c\neq 3$  can be dealt similarly as in the previous solution.

Let  $f(x) = x + g(x)$  then
\begin{align*}
    x^3 + y^3 + z^3 - 3xyz + y^2 g(y) + zg(z^2) \geq 3g(xyz) 
\end{align*}
Substituting  $(x,y,z)=(0,0,0)$  give  $g(0)\leq 0$ . We will show that  $g(x)=0$  for all  $x\neq 0$ . 

Substituting  $(x,y,z)=(1,1,1)$  gives  $g(1)\leq 0$ .

Since  $x^3+y^3+z^3 - 3xyz = (x+y+z) (x^2+y^2+z^2-xy-yz-zx)$ , substituting  $x=-(y+z)$  gives 
\begin{align*}
    y^2g(y)+zg(z^2)\ge 3g(-yz(y+z))
\end{align*}
for all  $y, z\in\mathbb{R}$ . Denote this statement by  $P(y,z)$ . $P(-2,1)$  gives  $4g(-2) + g(1) \geq 3g(-2) \Rightarrow g(-2)\geq -g(1)\geq  0$ .  $P(1,1)$  gives  $g(1) + g(1) \geq 3g(-2) \Rightarrow g(-2) \leq 0 \Rightarrow g(1)=g(-2)=0$ . $P(y,z)$  and  $P(-y,-z)$  gives
\begin{align*}
    y^2 g(y) + zg(z^2) &\geq 3g(-yz(y+z)) \tag{1}
    y^2 g(-y) - zg(z^2) &\geq 3g(yz(y+z)) \tag{2}
\end{align*} $(1)+(2):$ \begin{align*}
    y^2 (g(y)+g(-y)) \geq 3 [g(yz(y+z)) + g(-yz(y+z))] \tag{3}
\end{align*}

For a fixed  $y$ , choose  $z$  so that  $yz(y+z) = y$ . this is possible as the equation  $yz^2+y^2z-y=0$ , when viewed as a quadratic in  $z$ , has discriminant  $\Delta=y^2-4(y)(-y)=5y^2\ge 0$  for all  $y$ . Then with this choice of  $z$ ,  $P(y,z)$  gives 
\begin{align*}
    (y^2-3)(g(y)+g(-y)) &\geq 0
    g(y) + g(-y) &\geq 0 \quad \forall |y|>\sqrt{3}
    g(y) + g(-y) &\leq 0 \quad \forall |y|<\sqrt{3} \tag{4}
\end{align*}
So  $g(-1)\leq 0$  since  $g(1)=0$  and  $1<\sqrt{3}$ . 

For any  $y\neq 0$ , pick  $z$  large enough in (3) so that  $|yz(y+z)|>\sqrt{3}$ , then
\begin{align*}
    g(y) + g(-y) &\geq 0 \quad \forall y\neq0 \tag{5}
\end{align*}
Hence  $g(-1)=0$  because  $g(-1)\le 0$  and  $g(1)=0$ .

Now,  $P(y,-z)$  gives
\begin{align*}
    y^2 g(y) - zg(z^2) \geq 3g(yz(y-z)) \tag{6}
\end{align*} $(1)+(6):$ \begin{align*}
    2y^2 g(y) \geq 3g(yz(y-z)) + 3g(-yz(y+z)) \tag{7}
\end{align*} $y=1$  in (7):
\begin{align*}
    0=2g(1)\geq 3g(-z(z-1)) + 3g(-z(z+1))\tag{8}
\end{align*} $y=-1$  in (7):
\begin{align*}
    0=2g(-1) \geq 3g(z(z+1)) + 3g(z(z-1)) \tag{9}
\end{align*} $(8)+(9)$ :
\begin{align*}
    [g(z(z-1))+g(-z(z-1))] + [g(z(z+1))+g(-z(z+1))] \leq 0 \tag{10}
\end{align*}
By (5), we have each of the terms are at least  $0$ , namely
\begin{align*}
    g(z(z-1))+g(-z(z-1)) \ge 0 \quad \forall z(z-1)\neq 0
    g(z(z+1))+g(-z(z+1)) \ge 0 \quad \forall z(z+1)\neq 0 \tag{11}
\end{align*}
So equality in (10) holds, and hence  $g(z(z+1))+g(-z(z+1))=0$  for all reals  $z$  with  $z(z+1)\neq 0$ .  

However, as the quadratic  $z(z+1)$  ranges through all reals in the interval  $[-\frac14, \infty)$ , then for every  $y>0$ , we have  $g(y)+g(-y)=0$  from (11). Swapping the roles of  $y$  and  $-y$ , then we get
\begin{align*}
    g(y)+g(-y) = 0 \quad \forall y \neq 0 \tag{12}
\end{align*}
Therefore (2) becomes
\begin{align*}
    -y^2g(y) - zg(z^2) &\geq -3g(-yz(y+z)) & \forall 0\not \in \{y,z,y+z\}
    y^2g(y) + zg(z^2) &\leq 3g(-yz(y+z)) & \forall 0\not \in \{y,z,y+z\}\tag{13}
\end{align*}
and thus, with (1),
\begin{align*}
    y^2g(y) + zg(z^2) &= 3g(-yz(y+z)) & \forall 0\not \in \{y,z,y+z\}\tag{14}
\end{align*}
Swapping  $y$  and  $z$  gives
\begin{align*}
    y^2g(y) + zg(z^2) &= z^2g(z) + yg(y^2) & \forall 0\not \in \{y,z,y+z\}
    \therefore x^2g(x) - xg(x^2) &= 0 & \forall 0 \not \in \{x,1,x+1\}
\end{align*}
As this is also true for  $x=-1$ , we have
\begin{align*}
    x^2g(x) &= xg(x^2)  \quad \forall x \neq 0\tag{15}
\end{align*} $P(0,|z|)$  and  $P(0 ,-|z|)$  gives
\begin{align*}
    |z|g(|z|^2) &\geq 3g(0) \quad \text{and} \quad -|z|g(|z|^2) \geq 3g(0)
    \therefore \frac{3g(0)}{|z|} &\leq g(z^2) \leq -\frac{3g(0)}{|z|}
    \therefore |g(z^2)| &\leq -3g(0) \quad \forall z>1
\end{align*}
Now, (15) can be transformed into
\begin{align*}
    x^N g(x) = xg(x^N)
\end{align*}
for any  $N=2^k \ (k=1,2,\cdots)$ .  
If  $x>1$ ,
\begin{align*}
    |g(x)| = \frac{|g(x^N)|}{x^{N-1}} \leq \frac{-3g(0)}{x^{N-1}} \xrightarrow[]{N\rightarrow \infty} 0
\end{align*}
and hence  $g(x) =0$  for all  $|x|\geq 1$ . Now plug in large  $z$  in  $(14)$  to get
\begin{align*}
    y^2g(y) + 0= 0 \quad \forall y \neq 0
\end{align*}
and hence  $g(x) \equiv 0$ , i.e.  $f(x) \equiv x$  for all  $x\neq 0$ .   Finally,  $f(0)=g(0)\leq 0$  can be any number.  $\blacksquare$ **Comment.** (Wong Jer Ren) If  $c<0$ , then there exist very pathological solutions. Any function which satisfies  $f(x)=x$  for all  $x\ge 0$  and  $f(x)\ge -kx$  for some  $\displaystyle k\ge -\frac{3}{c}$  for all  $x<0$  can be checked to work, because for all  $x, y, z$ ,   $$ x^3+y^2f(y)+zf(z^2)=x^3+y^2f(y)+z^3\ge x^3+y^3+z^3\ge 3xyz $$   So if  $xyz\ge 0$ , then   $$ x^3+y^2f(y)+zf(z^2)\ge 3xyz\ge 0\ge cf(xyz) $$   and if  $xyz< 0$ , then   $$ x^3+y^2f(y)+zf(z^2)\ge 3xyz\ge -kc(xyz) \ge cf(xyz). $$

