There's a typo in the question: it should be  $\tfrac{1}{BC} - \tfrac{{\color{red}2}}{DC} = \tfrac{{\color{red}1}}{CA}$  instead.

Define  $\alpha=\angle BAC$ , so  $\cos(2\alpha)=\frac{AC}{CD}$ .

By the sine rule in  $\triangle ABC, \ \ \frac{1}{BC}=\frac{1}{AC}\frac{\sin(3\alpha)}{\sin(\alpha)}$ .

Expanding:\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{BC} = \frac{1}{AC} \frac{\sin(3\alpha)}{\sin(\alpha)}
&= \frac{1}{AC}\left( \frac{\sin(\alpha)\cos(2\alpha)+\sin(2\alpha)\cos(\alpha)}{\sin(\alpha)} \right) 
&= \frac{1}{AC}\left( \cos(2\alpha)+2\cos^2(\alpha) \right) 
&= \frac{1}{AC}\left( 1+2\cos(2\alpha) \right) 
&= \frac{1}{AC} + \frac{2}{CD}\quad\blacksquare
\end{align*}

Ooh, yuck!**Lemma:**\[ \sum_{x=1}^{n} x 2^x=(n-1)2^{n+1}+2 \]**Proof:**\begin{align*}\sum_{x=1}^{n} x 2^{x}
&\ =\  \sum_{x=1}^{n}\left( 2^{x}+\ldots+2^{n} \right) \ =\  \sum_{x=1}^{n}\left( 2^{n+1} - 2^{x} \right) 
&\ =\  n2^{n+1} - \sum_{x=1}^{n} 2^{x} \ =\  n2^{n+1} - (2^{n+1} - 2) 
&\ =\  (n-1)2^{n+1}+2\end{align*}

Let  $L_A(m)$  be the sum of  $M_A$  over subsets whose largest element is  $m$ .

Iterating over smallest elements  $k=1,\ldots,m$ , this is:
\begin{align*}
L_A(m) &= \sum_{k=1}^{m} (m-k) 2^{m-k-1} 
&= \sum_{x=1}^{m-1} x 2^{x-1} 
&= (m-2)2^{m-1}+1\ \ \text{by the lemma}
\end{align*}
Then\begin{align*}
M_A
&= \sum_{m=1}^n L_A(m) 
&= \sum_{m=1}^n \left( (m-2)2^{m-1}+1 \right) 
&= \sum_{m=3}^n \left( (m-2)2^{m-1} \right) + n-1 
&= 2\left( (n-3)2^{n-1}+2 \right) + n-1\ \ \text{by the lemma} 
&= \boxed{(n-3)2^n+n+3}
\end{align*}

